  "passages": [
    {
      "id": "pcp-expository-opening",
      "section": "Introduction",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "The same holds for every decision problem whose answer is yes exactly\nwhen some polynomial-length **witness** is accepted by a deterministic\npolynomial-time algorithm.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.np_subset_pcp"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "Introduction",
        "block": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-expository-theorem",
      "section": "2",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "**Theorem 2.1 (PCP theorem; [ALMSS98], [AS98]).** For every\n$\\mathcal L\\in\\mathrm{NP}$, there are a polynomial-time verifier $V$,\nnatural-number constants $\\alpha,\\beta,\\ell$, and a polynomial $p$, such\nthat on every input $x$ of length $n$:",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.np_subset_pcp"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "2. The PCP theorem",
        "block": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-expository-colour-polynomials",
      "section": "4",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "Then $\\chi$ describes a proper colouring precisely when\n$\\operatorname{Val}$ vanishes on $H^m$ and\n$\\operatorname{Prop}$ vanishes on $H^{2m}$. The adjacency factor makes\nthe second requirement automatic for nonedges.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.ThreeColorGrid.constraints_iff_satisfies"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "4. Colourings as polynomial equations",
        "block": 12
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-expository-grid-certificate",
      "section": "5",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "More generally, by the\nCombinatorial Nullstellensatz in the form of [ABSSW26, Lemma 2.9], every\npolynomial vanishing on $H^m$ has a representation",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.GridVanishing.vanishesOn_iff_exists_decomposition"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "5. Vanishing certificates",
        "block": 1
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-expository-digit-encoding",
      "section": "6",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "If $q\\geq10^6d^3$\nand $\\delta\\leq1/(4\\cdot10^6)$, then $f$ is $2\\delta$-close to a\npolynomial of total degree at most $d$.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.PointLineTest.exists_close_of_small_rejection"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "6. Low-degree tests and the set-multilinear encoding",
        "block": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-expository-weighted-test",
      "section": "6",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "**Theorem 6.2 (standard low-degree test; cf. [ABSSW26, Lemma 3.3]).** With\nthese generator nodes and $q>2c$, if the test rejects with probability\n$\\delta<1/[2(c+2)^2]$, then $f$ is $2\\delta$-close to a polynomial of\ntotal degree at most $c$.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.GeneratorLowDegreeTest.exists_close"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "The standard low-degree test and local correction",
        "block": 4
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-expository-hadamard",
      "section": "7",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "If the BLR linearity test rejects a word with\nprobability $\\varepsilon$, the word is $\\varepsilon$-close to a linear\nfunctional.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.BLR.exists_linearMap_distance_le_of_rejection_le"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "7. The Hadamard encoding",
        "block": 9
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-expository-cubic-labels",
      "section": "7",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "Lee's\ndegree-three Hadamard test reads ten bits; their test HadTest reads\neight, retaining only the top-degree product check.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.Hadamard.testQueries_length"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "Labels of degree three",
        "block": 13
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-expository-binary-tests",
      "section": "8",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "then there is one polynomial of degree at most $D$ whose encoding is\nwithin $\\varepsilon=S\\kappa$ of the received points table on both\nfull and homogeneous labels.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.EncodedLineLowDegreeTest.exists_close"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "The stored proof",
        "block": 19
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-expository-assembly",
      "section": "9",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "For soundness, fix an arbitrary proof and let $\\kappa$ be the seven-check\nverifier's rejection rate, so that each check rejects with probability at\nmost $\\kappa$.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.ThreeColorVerifier.rejectionRate_ge_of_not_colorable"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "9. Soundness of the seven-check verifier",
        "block": 6
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-expository-resources",
      "section": "10",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "Lee also implements the field, arithmetic,\nadjacency extension, queries and acceptance predicate in polynomial time\non a concrete machine model; this proof is in the [full argument][long].",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Lang.ThreeColorRuntime.polyTimeAlg"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "10. Randomness, proof length and repetition",
        "block": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "linked-source-14",
      "section": "9",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "For perfect completeness, interpolate a proper colouring, form its\ndifference polynomial, construct both vanishing certificates, and supply\ntheir encodings and lines tables. All seven checks then pass for every\nseed, given the degree-capacity condition $5m(h-1)<b^c$ verified in\nAppendix A.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.ThreeColorVerifier.exists_proof_rejectionRate_eq_zero"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "9. Soundness of the seven-check verifier",
        "block": 5
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "linked-source-16",
      "section": "8",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "The **ideal test**\nreads the values of the nearby polynomials in place of the received\nanswers; a failed grid condition forces it to reject through Lemma 3.1,\nand the cost of the replacement is bounded in Section 9.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.BinaryZeroOnGridCorrection.rejectionRate_ge"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "The stored proof",
        "block": 33
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "linked-source-17",
      "section": "2",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "Perfect completeness\nsupplies such bits on yes-instances and soundness excludes them on\nno-instances, all in polynomial time, so the problem is in NP.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.pcp_subset_np_concrete"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "2. The PCP theorem",
        "block": 8
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "linked-source-18",
      "section": "7",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "Encodings of\ndistinct messages differ at exactly half the positions: their difference\nis a nonzero **linear functional**, a map $x\\mapsto s'\\cdot x$ with\n$s'\\ne0$, and translation by a vector on which it\nis one pairs each zero with a one.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.Hadamard.relativeDistance_linearEncode"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "7. The Hadamard encoding",
        "block": 5
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "linked-source-19",
      "section": "Appendix A. Parameters and error bounds",
      "role": "explains",
      "quote": "For the implemented verifier, fair-bit\nsampling leaves rejection probability at least\n$\\gamma=1/1137847282503168$ on every proof of a no-instance. The fixed\nrepetition count $R=1137847282503168$ satisfies $R\\gamma=1$.",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.ThreeColorAmplifiedVerifier.verification_guarantees"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "Parameter calculation",
        "block": 9
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-expository-homogeneous-encoding",
      "role": "defines",
      "quote": "$$\nh_s(x)=s\\cdot x\\pmod2.\n$$",
      "lean": [
        "PCP.Algebraic.Hadamard.linearEncode"
      ],
      "anchor": {
        "heading": "7. The Hadamard encoding",
        "block": 2
      },
      "section": "7"
    },
    {
      "id": "pcp-expository-clause-flags",
      "role": "defines",
      "quote": "$$\nz_0=0,\\qquad z_{i+1}=z_i\\lor(x_i\\mathbin{\\mathrm{xor}}p_i),\n\\qquad R(p)\\lor z_k=1.\n$$",
